                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi></head>
                <p>On May 9, 1954, Brigadier General Charles H.
                        Bonesteel III, Defense Member on the Planning Board of the
                    National Security Council, addressed a memorandum to Charles E. Wilson, the Secretary of Defense, titled “Future U.S.
                    Action Regarding Indo-China.” For text, see <hi rendition="#italic">United
                        States–Vietnam Relations, 1945–1967</hi>, Book 9, page 442. <pb facs="0140"
                        n="1522" xml:id="pg1522"/> The General prepared a second memorandum, titled
                    “A Concept for Action with Regard to Indochina,” which was submitted to the
                    Planning Board on May 13. For text, see <hi rendition="#italic">United
                        States–Vietnam Relations, 1945–1967</hi>, Book 9, pages 460–461. A copy in
                    the Eisenhower Library bears the
                    handwritten notation “Bonesteel May 11,
                    1954.” (Eisenhower Library, White House
                    Office, “Project ‘Clean Up’”)</p>
            </div>
